# Task FEATURE-011-US-002-T02 — Test & verify: Author metadata for all sliders

> **DaemonCore coding-agent prompt.** Work type: `feature` · Size: `S` · Priority: `P3` · Status: `todo`

## Where this fits
- **Epic EPIC-011 — Policy Slider Intelligence.** Turn each policy slider into an educational tool: description, intended effect, likely side effects, uncertainty level, affected score categories and real-world examples, surfaced via progressive disclosure.
- **Feature FEATURE-011 — Policy Slider Intelligence — implementation.**
- **Story FEATURE-011-US-002 — Author metadata for all sliders.** _As a user, I want each slider explained so I understand its purpose and effects._

## Objective
Add automated tests and verify acceptance for: Author metadata for all sliders.

## What to do
Write **deterministic automated tests** that cover each acceptance criterion of the parent story:
- Assert: All sliders have description, intended effect, side effects, uncertainty, categories, examples
Use fixed, hand-computed numbers (see `tests/test_fiscal_model.py`). Avoid randomness, network calls and time-dependence.

## Acceptance criteria (Definition of Done)
- [ ] Automated tests cover the story's acceptance criteria
- [ ] Tests are deterministic and pass in CI
- [ ] New/updated tests pass and cover the above
- [ ] No regression in the existing `pytest` suite

## Dependencies
Complete these first: `FEATURE-011-US-002-T01`.

## Project conventions
- Target Python 3.13. Use British spelling in all user-facing text.
- Keep the pure model layer (`model/`) free of Streamlit; `app.py` is UI/render only.
- Pin any new runtime deps in `requirements.txt`; test-only deps in `requirements-dev.txt`.
- Write deterministic `pytest` tests; follow the style of `tests/test_fiscal_model.py`.
- Match the surrounding code style. Prefer small, coherent diffs over broad refactors.
- Update `README.md` / `docs/` whenever behaviour changes.

## Guardrails (EPIC-011)
- Slider metadata is data (not code), keyed by lever id.
- State uncertainty explicitly; keep claims neutral and sourced.
- Use progressive disclosure so the UI stays uncluttered.

## Verification
- `python3 -m py_compile <changed .py files>`
- `python3 -m pytest -q` — all tests pass (add/extend tests for this change).
- Boot headless once: `streamlit run app.py --server.headless true` and confirm it loads without error.

## Notes
- This simulator is a transparent scenario sandbox, not an official forecast — keep that framing in any user-facing text.
- If requirements are ambiguous or you must change a public interface, stop and flag it rather than guessing.
